q(2,'🟡','open_lock',"Open the lock (BFS over an implicit graph). A lock has four wheels, each showing a digit 0-9; one move turns one wheel one step up or down and 9 and 0 wrap around. It starts at '0000'. `deadends` is a list of up to 500 four-digit strings that jam the lock (if you reach one you cannot continue from it) and `target` is a four-digit string. Return the minimum number of moves to reach `target`, or -1 if impossible (also -1 if '0000' itself is a dead end; if `target` is a dead end it is unreachable). Describe the implicit graph (10^4 states, 8 neighbours each) and why you do not build it up front.",
 [{'deadends':['0201','0101','0102','1212','2002'],'target':'0202'},{'deadends':['8888'],'target':'0009'},{'deadends':['0000'],'target':'8888'},{'deadends':['8887','8889','8878','8898','8788','8988','7888','9888'],'target':'8888'}],GG+' | '+ED)
q(2,'🟡','dfs_topo',"Topological sort by DFS (reverse postorder). `n` nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^5) and `edges` a list of directed [u, v] (0 <= len <= 2*10^5; self loops possible). Run an iterative three-colour DFS: loop over start nodes 0, 1, ..., n-1 in ascending order, explore each node's out-edges in input order, append a node to `post` when it finishes, and return `post` reversed. If any edge reaches a node currently on the DFS path, return []. This order is deterministic, and the checker accepts exactly this list. Explain why reverse postorder puts every edge u -> v with u before v, and why the order generally differs from Kahn's. "+REC,
 [{'n':4,'edges':[[0,1],[0,2],[1,3],[2,3]]},{'n':3,'edges':[[0,1],[1,2],[2,0]]},{'n':3,'edges':[]},{'n':5,'edges':[[3,1],[4,1],[4,0],[2,3]]}],GG+' | '+ED)
q(2,'🔴','sliding_puzzle',"Sliding puzzle (state-space BFS). The board is a 2 x 3 grid holding the numbers 0-5 once each, where 0 is the blank. One move slides a tile that is up, down, left or right of the blank into it. Return the minimum number of moves to reach [[1,2,3],[4,5,0]], or -1 if it is unreachable. Treat each board as one node (flatten it to a tuple) and BFS; state the number of states (720), why exactly half of them are unreachable, and why the same code on a bigger board needs a smarter search.",
 [{'board':[[1,2,3],[4,0,5]]},{'board':[[1,2,3],[5,4,0]]},{'board':[[4,1,2],[5,0,3]]},{'board':[[1,2,3],[4,5,0]]}],GG+' | '+ED)
q(2,'🔴','alien_order',"Alien dictionary (topological order from constraints). `words` is a list of 1 to 100 non-empty lowercase words (length <= 100) claimed to be sorted in an alien alphabet's lexicographic order (a proper prefix comes before the longer word). Return a string containing each distinct letter that appears in `words` exactly once, in an order under which `words` is sorted, or '' if no such order exists (a cycle among the constraints, or a longer word placed before its own prefix). Many alphabets can be valid: the checker accepts any string that contains each letter once and under which the list is sorted, and accepts '' only if no ordering of the letters works; the example shows one accepted answer. Explain how the first differing letters of adjacent words give edges, and why only adjacent pairs are needed.",
 [{'words':['wrt','wrf','er','ett','rftt']},{'words':['z','x','z']},{'words':['abc','ab']},{'words':['a','b','a']} ,{'words':['ab']}],GG+' | '+ED)
q(2,'🔴','cheapest_flights',"Cheapest flights with at most k stops. `n` cities 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 100), `flights` a list of directed [u, v, price] (price 1 <= price <= 10^4, up to 5000 flights) and integers `src`, `dst`, `k` with 0 <= k < n. Return the lowest total price of a route from `src` to `dst` using at most k intermediate cities (so at most k + 1 flights), or -1 if none. Use Bellman-Ford for k + 1 rounds, each round relaxing from a copy of the previous round's distances, and explain why using the same array in-place can allow too many flights per round and why plain Dijkstra with a visited set can give a wrong answer here.",
 [{'n':4,'flights':[[0,1,100],[1,2,100],[2,0,100],[1,3,600],[2,3,200]],'src':0,'dst':3,'k':1},{'n':3,'flights':[[0,1,100],[1,2,100],[0,2,500]],'src':0,'dst':2,'k':0},{'n':3,'flights':[[0,1,100],[1,2,100],[0,2,500]],'src':0,'dst':2,'k':1},{'n':2,'flights':[],'src':0,'dst':1,'k':0}],GG+' | '+ED)

# ================= 1 star
q(1,'🟡','floyd_city',"Floyd-Warshall: the city with the fewest neighbours within a distance threshold. `n` cities 0..n-1 (2 <= n <= 100), `edges` a list of undirected [u, v, w] with 1 <= w <= 10^4 (up to 4000; repeats possible) and an integer `threshold`. For each city count the other cities whose shortest-path distance is at most `threshold`. Return the city with the smallest count, and the largest index if several tie. Use the O(n^3) all-pairs dynamic programme (loop k outermost) and explain why k must be the outer loop and when you would run Dijkstra from every node instead.",
 [{'n':4,'edges':[[0,1,3],[1,2,1],[1,3,4],[2,3,1]],'threshold':4},{'n':3,'edges':[],'threshold':5},{'n':5,'edges':[[0,1,2],[0,4,8],[1,2,3],[1,4,2],[2,3,1],[3,4,1]],'threshold':2}],GG+' | '+ED)
q(1,'🟡','euler',"Eulerian path and circuit: existence only. `n` nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 20) and `edges` a list of undirected [u, v] (0 <= len <= 40; repeats and self loops allowed, a self loop adds 2 to its node's degree). A trail uses every edge exactly once. Return 'circuit' if some trail uses every edge exactly once and ends where it starts, 'path' if one exists but none closes, and 'none' if no such trail exists (a graph with no edges counts as 'circuit'; isolated nodes do not matter). Decide it from degrees (the number of odd-degree nodes) and the connectivity of the nodes that have edges, without constructing the trail; say why the Hierholzer construction is O(E) but is not needed here.",
 [{'n':4,'edges':[[0,1],[1,2],[2,0],[2,3],[3,2]]},{'n':4,'edges':[[0,1],[1,2],[2,3]]},{'n':4,'edges':[[0,1],[0,2],[0,3]]},{'n':5,'edges':[[0,1],[2,3]]},{'n':3,'edges':[]}],GG+' | '+ED)
q(1,'🟡','prim_points',"Prim's algorithm on a dense graph: connect points at minimum cost. `points` is a list of n distinct integer points [x, y] (1 <= n <= 1000, |coordinate| <= 10^6); the cost between two points is their Manhattan distance |x1 - x2| + |y1 - y2|, and every pair may be connected. Return the minimum total cost of edges that connect all points. Use Prim in O(n^2) with a plain distance array (no heap, no edge list of about 500000 edges) and say when a heap-based Prim or Kruskal is better; state why the answer is a minimum spanning tree weight and therefore unique even if the trees are not.",
 [{'points':[[0,0],[2,2],[3,10],[5,2],[7,0]]},{'points':[[3,12],[-2,5],[-4,1]]},{'points':[[0,0]]}],GG+' | '+ED)
q(1,'🟡','dfs_marking',"Iterative DFS: mark on pop versus mark on push. `edges` is a list of undirected [u, v] on nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^5, no self loops or repeats), adjacency lists in input order as in the BFS/DFS-orders question, and `start` a node. Return `[A, B]`. A: stack = [start]; pop u; skip it if already visited; mark and record u; push its neighbours in reverse list order. This is exactly the recursive preorder. B: mark start as seen when pushed; pop u and record it; for each neighbour v in list order, if v is not yet seen, mark it and push it. Both visit the same set of nodes but B can give a different order that is not a true DFS preorder. Explain why, and which variant keeps the stack smaller.",
 [{'n':4,'edges':[[0,1],[0,2],[1,3],[2,3]],'start':0},{'n':4,'edges':[[0,1],[1,2],[2,3]],'start':0},{'n':3,'edges':[[0,1],[0,2],[1,2]],'start':0}],GG+' | '+ED)
q(1,'🟡','stones',"Most stones removed (union-find on rows and columns). `stones` is a list of n distinct integer points [row, col] (1 <= n <= 1000, 0 <= row, col <= 10^4). A stone can be removed if another stone that has not been removed shares its row or its column. Return the maximum number of stones that can be removed. Show that the answer is n minus the number of connected groups when stones sharing a row or column are linked, with rows and columns as union-find nodes.",
 [{'stones':[[0,0],[0,1],[1,0],[1,2],[2,1],[2,2]]},{'stones':[[0,0],[0,2],[1,1],[2,0],[2,2]]},{'stones':[[0,0]]}],GG+' | '+ED)
q(1,'🟡','dag_shortest',"Shortest paths in a DAG with negative weights. `n` nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^5) and `edges` a list of directed [u, v, w] with -100 <= w <= 100 forming a directed acyclic graph (guaranteed, up to 2*10^5 edges, repeats allowed) and `src`. Return the list of shortest distances from `src` (None for unreachable nodes) in O(V + E): process nodes in topological order and relax their out-edges. Explain why this beats Bellman-Ford and why Dijkstra would be wrong with negative edges.",
 [{'n':5,'edges':[[0,1,2],[0,2,6],[1,2,-3],[2,3,1],[1,3,4]],'src':0},{'n':3,'edges':[[1,2,-5]],'src':0},{'n':1,'edges':[],'src':0}],GG+' | '+ED)
q(1,'🟡','safe_states',"Eventual safe nodes. `graph` is a directed graph in adjacency-list form on nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 10^4): `graph[u]` lists the nodes u points to (up to 4*10^4 entries in total; repeats and self loops allowed). A node is terminal if it has no outgoing edge and safe if every path starting there reaches a terminal node (so a node that can reach a cycle is unsafe). Return the ascending list of safe nodes. Solve it by running Kahn's algorithm on the reversed graph and explain why that works.",
 [{'graph':[[1,2],[2,3],[5],[0],[5],[],[]]},{'graph':[[1,2,3,4],[1,2],[3,4],[0,4],[]]},{'graph':[[0]]},{'graph':[[]]}],GG+' | '+ED)
q(1,'🟡','dijkstra_negative',"Why Dijkstra fails with a negative edge. `edges` are directed [u, v, w] on nodes 0..n-1 (1 <= n <= 8, -10 <= w <= 10) with no negative cycle reachable from `src`. Run the finalise-on-first-pop version of Dijkstra: dist[src] = 0, heap of (distance, node) with (0, src); pop (d, u); skip it if u is already finalised; finalise u; for each edge (u, v, w) in input order, if v is NOT finalised and d + w < dist[v], set dist[v] = d + w and push (d + w, v). Return `[dijkstra_dist, true_dist]` with None for unreachable nodes, where true_dist comes from Bellman-Ford. Give a three-node graph where the two differ and explain the exact step at which Dijkstra's reasoning (a finalised distance cannot be improved) breaks; mention that the stale-check variant, which does re-expand nodes, is correct on these graphs but has no good running-time guarantee.",
 [{'n':3,'edges':[[0,1,4],[0,2,5],[2,1,-3]],'src':0},{'n':3,'edges':[[0,1,1],[1,2,2]],'src':0},{'n':4,'edges':[[0,1,2],[0,2,3],[2,1,-2],[1,3,1]],'src':0}],PQ+' | '+GG)
